"Compares two population groups across ordered age bands with mirrored bars sharing a central origin.\n\nUse when: Age structure must be compared between two clearly defined populations. Counts or population shares use a declared, comparable denominator.\n\nAvoid when: The groups require unrelated scales or the ordered bands are not meaningful.\n\nVisual grammar: Vertical positions encode ordered age bands. Left and right bar lengths encode group counts or percentages using mirrored positive magnitudes.\n\nBuild: Create identical age bands for both groups and include any open-ended final band. Choose counts or shares and state the population denominator. Draw mirrored zero-based bars with age labels in a protected central lane.\n\nCheck: Check both sides use the same magnitude scale and band widths. Verify displayed percentages use the intended group or overall denominator. Label age-band boundaries and reference date clearly.\n\nPitfalls: Left-side negative coordinates are layout mechanics, not negative populations. Overlapping age bands or inconsistent open-ended intervals distort the structure.\n\nExample: Compare two synthetic resident groups across ten-year age bands with mirrored counts and a common count axis.\n\nAlternatives: Grouped bar chart, Histogram, Diverging bar chart."
